A||||8||ChadMan||No parking brake = shorter caliper life?||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||09-25-2000||03:16 PM||||I installed new calipers and a new front parking brake cable in my 88 Coupe a few months back, but never got around to replacing the equalizer and two rear cables, so I still have no emergency brake.  I don't really miss the e-brake that much, but plan to fix it eventually.<P>My question is this:  I've heard some people say if you never use your parking brake, you'll screw up your rear calipers.  Yet I've heard other people (usually automatic Fiero owners) say that's baloney.  What do you guys think?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||09-25-2000||03:38 PM||||88's have a much better e brake set-up, the cables are less prone to rusting amd melting. The reason you should always use the e-brake is really to keep the cables and associated parts freed up and no using the e-brake will not screw up your calipers.||||reg||1
Z||000002||wwh0001||09-25-2000||03:47 PM||||The emergency brake is what keeps your rear brakes adjusted properly. If never used your rear brakes will not work properly putting most pressure on your front breaks which means your front pads will wear out quicker. Your total breaking power will be diminshed as well. If your brake pedal is a bit "spongy" pull your e-brake  a couple of times and you should feel a better pedal response. Pulling the e-brake racthets out the pad to the rotor for adjsutment. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DjKid||09-25-2000||03:57 PM||||I dont understand why not using your ebrake would diminish breaking power, would that meamn that these GA brake upgrades would actually not be as good as they are on the GA cuz they dont have the ebrake? could you perhaps explain this further? and make it something I can believe?<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||09-25-2000||04:00 PM||||Well, on my Nissan Maxima, if you pulled the e-brake a few times, it would adjust the pads in a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000005||wwh0001||09-25-2000||04:10 PM||||Sure, its simple. your rear calipers piston actually rotates as is pushes the pad against the rotor. Your front calipers do not. If the e-brake is never used then the piston/pad distance will not be optimal. so when you depress the brake pedal your hydraulic system will have to move the rear pad further. your front pads will be pushed against the rotor harder making them wear faster and your total braking power will not be as effective as it would if your rear brakes were properly adjusted. And yes any caliper that was designed to have the e-brake as their adjustment will not be as effective if there is no e- brake. The total difference would be negligable in most applications. The <BR>only real disadvantage is your front pads wear quicker.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||09-25-2000||06:09 PM||||First, the front brakes do much more of the work anyway.  And second, the GA brakes weren't made for an E brake.  What you're using is 2 sets of <B>front</B> brakes from the GA/Beretta.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||09-26-2000||09:11 AM||||Thanks, you guys.  Fixing the parking brake will cost me only $50 worth of parts and a couple hours' work, but I was thinking of putting it off until spring.  But if I run the risk of losing rear braking power or prematurely wearing out my front pads, I'll fix the e-brake before the end of the month.||||reg||4
Z||000008||William Federle||09-26-2000||01:24 PM||||It will be $50.00 worth of parts if the brake levers on your rear calipers move freely. If not, it might be a bit more to get new calipers or rebuild kits - I had to get new front and both rear cables as well as two new calipers for the rear to make my e-brake work.<P>good luck||||reg||1
